//!
//! # OptiX Acceleration Boundary
//!
//! ## What Changed
//!
//! OptiX is no longer owned only as an internal implementation detail of
//! `lbm_3d_cuda`.
//!
//! A new crate, `gororoba_optix`, now owns the generic acceleration boundary:
//!
//! - dynamic loading of `libnvoptix.so.1`
//! - OptiX function-table query
//! - current-CUDA-context device-context creation
//! - current-CUDA-context device-context destruction
//! - the minimal FFI type surface shared by downstream users
//!
//! `lbm_3d_cuda` now consumes that crate instead of defining its own private
//! runtime/FFI layer.
//!
//! ## Why This Boundary Matters
//!
//! Before this change, the LBM CUDA crate owned both:
//!
//! - generic OptiX runtime concerns
//! - LBM-specific particle tracing and BVH semantics
//!
//! That made it harder to reason about what was reusable acceleration substrate and
//! what was domain logic. It also made future reuse by non-LBM GPU workloads much
//! more awkward.
//!
//! The new split is:
//!
//! - `gororoba_optix`
//! - reusable runtime and FFI boundary
//! - `lbm_3d_cuda::optix_pipeline`
//! - LBM-aware pipeline assembly and source management
//! - `lbm_3d_cuda::optix_tracer`
//! - LBM-specific SBT payloads, density thresholds, and brick semantics
//! - `lbm_3d_cuda::optix_orchestrator`
//! - LBM/OptiX stream choreography for Eulerian-Lagrangian workflows
//!
//! ## What Is Generic vs LBM-Specific
//!
//! ### Generic and reusable
//!
//! - shared-library discovery
//! - ABI/function-table loading
//! - OptiX FFI structs and handles
//! - live OptiX runtime tied to current CUDA context
//! - probe path for "is OptiX available here?"
//!
//! ### LBM-specific and intentionally left in `lbm_3d_cuda`
//!
//! - `LbmSbtData`
//! - brick occupancy derived from LBM density fields
//! - tracer BVH rebuild thresholds based on fluid occupancy
//! - particle advection semantics over LBM velocity fields
//! - `optix_tracer.cu` and `optix_brick_scan.cu`
//!
//! ## What This Enables Next
//!
//! This boundary makes the next GPU-stack work cleaner:
//!
//! 1. viewer or particle tools can depend on `gororoba_optix` without pulling in
//! >  the whole LBM science stack
//! 2. a future generic ray-acceleration crate can layer on top of
//! >  `gororoba_optix`
//! 3. `lbm_3d_cuda` can keep evolving domain-specific OptiX science without also
//! >  owning the loader/runtime substrate
//!
//! ## What Is Still Not Done
//!
//! This is a real improvement, but not the full end state yet.
//!
//! Still to do:
//!
//! - extract generic OptiX pipeline-building helpers only if another workload
//! beyond LBM actually needs them
//! - define a backend-neutral viewer frame contract that can surface OptiX-backed
//! particle views alongside CUDA or Vulkan volume views
//! - decide whether any of the current OptiX launch/build orchestration should move
//! to a second reusable crate or remain solver-local
//!
//! ## Design Rule Going Forward
//!
//! If a type or function can exist without knowing anything about:
//!
//! - LBM density fields
//! - LBM velocity buffers
//! - tracer particle semantics
//! - brick occupancy thresholds
//!
//! then it belongs in `gororoba_optix` or another reusable GPU substrate crate,
//! not in `lbm_3d_cuda`.
//!
